bevy-horizon: turn on NAK_DEBUG's measured flags by default in Switch builds

For mesa-switch (https://github.com/danfromtico/mesa-switch, by danfromtico and NaGaa95; MIT)
at 606441c9, after 0001-0018. scripts/build_nvk.sh applies it; this text before the first
"diff --git" line is ignored by git apply.

This series' NAK_DEBUG flags xbar, sink, remat, xbrk, lsink and plat (patches 0004, 0005, 0007,
0011 and 0015) change how NAK compiles for Maxwell and older GPUs, and each made Skate 3's
frames faster on a Switch Lite (findings 21, 22, 30, 33 and 38). Switch builds
now start with all six on, and "-name" in NAK_DEBUG turns one off, so NAK_DEBUG=-plat,-lsink
turns off two. ubra (patch 0006) stays off, since it was never measured. Other builds start
with none on, as upstream does, and NAK_DEBUG works there as before, except that "-name" turns
a flag off instead of being reported as unknown, and empty items are skipped.

meson sets the Rust cfg nak_switch_defaults for horizon builds: NAK is compiled for
aarch64-unknown-linux-gnu on the Switch too, so target_os can't tell the builds apart. NAK reads
NAK_DEBUG once, when NVK creates its compiler, and a Switch build then prints the flags in
effect on stderr. The flags key NVK's shader cache (nak_debug_flags, in nvk_shader.c), so
shaders compiled before and after this change are cached apart.

Two tests in api.rs check the flag table against DebugFlags and how "name" and "-name" apply.
On a PC, with patch 0016, they pass, as do NAK's other 16 tests that meson runs.

